{"type":"text","timestamp":1777646505189,"sessionID":"ses_21c034101ffeFPq0hfIu7h3FeU","part":{"id":"prt_de3fcd7c3001e6gEENM9EcMyKS","messageID":"msg_de3fcd277001yogbCNutwkA1fA","sessionID":"ses_21c034101ffeFPq0hfIu7h3FeU","type":"text","text":"Now I need to analyze how Jews (who represent 70%+ of the world's Jewish population) and Italians may be genetically similar, extracting relevant factors from the documents I've read.\n</think>\n\nBased on the documents provided, I can answer your question about the genetic similarity between Jews (particularly Ashkenazi Jews who represent at least 70% of Jews worldwide) and Italians.\n\n## Two Factors for Genetic Similarity Between Jews and Italians:\n\n1. **Shared Mediterranean (Southern European) Genes from Host Population Admixture**: According to genetic studies, Jews and Italians share a common genetic heritage with significant Middle Eastern ancestry - specifically around 50-80% of DNA from the Ashkenazi Y chromosome originated in the Near East (Paragraph 31). More importantly, for modern Italians of Iberian and North African origin who have converted to Judaism and Jewish populations, Behar et al. noted that Ashkenazi and Sephardi Jews (particularly when considering closely related groups) show genetic similarity because \"the source of non-Jewish admixture is mainly southern European\" (Paragraph 37).\n\n2. **Historical Inter-marriage and Conversions During the Roman Empire**: A 2010 study by Atzmon-Ostrer et al. found that \"the population most closely related to Ashkenazi Jews are modern-day Italians.\" The study speculated that the genetic similarity between Ashkenazi Jews and Italians may be due to \"inter-marriage and conversions in the time of the Roman Empire\" (Paragraph 65). The study also found that with respect to non-Jewish European groups, modern-day Italians are the most closely related to Ashkenazi Jews, and the genetic markers suggest similar origins for European Jewry, refuting major Central and Eastern European or Slavic genetic contributions to the formation of Ashkenazi Jewry (Paragraph 65).","time":{"start":1777646505180,"end":1777646505180}}}
